// The stdlib LOADER — it enumerates every `.maxon` file under the checkout's `stdlib/`, top level and
// subdirectories alike, and registers each one into the query database exactly as a user source is —
// plus ONE temporary thing: a WHITELIST that filters which of those files are actually loaded while
// shv2 grows the language module by module.
//
// ⭐ **THE WHITELIST IS SCAFFOLDING AND IT IS MEANT TO DIE. THE LOADER IS NOT.** v1
// (`StdlibLoader.PrependStdlib`) and the bootstrap (`BuildCache.WithStdlibSources`) load ALL of
// `stdlib/`, and that is shv2's end state too — most of those files simply do not compile with shv2 yet
// (Map/FilePath/String-methods each have their own blockers, see PLAN.md). The filter bounds the blast
// radius until they do.
//
// It is written as a FILTER INSIDE the real loader, not as a list the loader walks, and that shape is
// the point: **the end state is a DELETION, not a rewrite.** Remove the three marked pieces below —
// the list, the membership test, and the `continue` that consults it — and what is left already loads
// all of `stdlib/`. A loader that walked a hardcoded list would have to be written from scratch on that
// day.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING OUTSIDE THIS FILE MAY KNOW A WHITELIST EXISTS.** Everything downstream deals in two
// durable facts — "is this function STDLIB source or USER source?" and "is it reachable from `main`?" —
// computed in `StdlibSource.maxon`, and both stay true when the filter goes. A pass that reasoned in
// terms of "whitelisted" would have to be rewritten that day; a pass that reasons in terms of stdlib
// source does not notice.
//
// ⭐ HOW A LOADED MODULE REACHES USER CODE. Each file is registered into the query database
// (`fileChanged`) exactly like a user source, so it flows through the SAME tokenize → signature-index →
// parse → merge spine with no query-spine change. Its declarations therefore populate `funcSignatures`,
// and a user program can call `Clock.nowMs()` though the program itself contains no Clock.
//
// ⭐ **LOAD ORDER: SORTED, AND FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN THE BOOTSTRAP'S.** The bootstrap sorts its
// stdlib file list (`0-Compiler.cs:967` — `Interfaces.maxon` first, then helper subdirectories, then
// top level) because ITS front end needs declarations to have been seen before a use is compiled.
// **shv2 does not have that constraint and this loader deliberately does not copy it**:
// `queryProgramSignatures` sweeps EVERY file's tokens for declarations before any file is parsed, so
// visibility is order-independent (ARCHITECTURE's query-spine section). What shv2 DOES share is the
// other half of the bootstrap's reason — a directory listing comes back in filesystem order, and an
// order that varies by machine would make the emitted function order, and therefore the committed
// goldens, vary with it. So the paths are sorted, byte-lexicographically, and nothing depends on which
// order that is beyond its being the same everywhere.
//
// ⚠ Two further things the bootstrap does that this does NOT, both deliberate:
//   • It EXCLUDES `stdlib/Internals.maxon` (the `__Internals.*` intrinsic namespace it does not
//     implement). shv2 does not implement it either, but the filter already excludes it and a second,
//     permanent exclusion for a case shv2 has not yet met would be an unmeasured guess. Whoever deletes
//     the filter meets `Internals.maxon` on that day and decides then.
//   • It CACHES the parsed stdlib module process-wide. shv2's caching is the query spine's
//     content-hash memo, which already covers these files because they are registered as ordinary
//     sources; a second cache would be the same fact stored twice.
//
// ⭐ THE ONE INVARIANT WHILE THE FILTER STANDS: adding a module changes NOTHING for a program that does
// not use it. A program that never calls Clock must compile to the exact bytes it did before Clock was
// listed — same x64 goldens, same wasm/arm64 output. Two mechanisms compose to guarantee that:
//
//   1. Dead-function elimination (`DeadFunctionElimination.eliminateDeadFunctions`) drops every stdlib
//      function no reachable code calls, at the target-neutral backend entry — so an unused Clock never
//      reaches isel/regalloc/encode and never lands in a golden, on any target.
//
//   2. ⚠ But DFE runs at the Std tier, AFTER `scanRuntimeUsage` has already decided which RUNTIME FLOOR
//      the program carries (heap / GT scheduler / wall clock) by walking the MERGED MAXON MODULE.
//      Clock.nowMs's body calls `__gt_now_ns` and WallClock.nowUnixSeconds's calls `__clock_now_unix_s`,
//      so a NAIVE load would make `scanRuntimeUsage` install the GT scheduler + the wall-clock `.data`
//      slots for a program that never reads a clock — slots DFE does not prune (it prunes FUNCTIONS,
//      never `.data`). MEASURED before that gate: a bare `return 7` grew from 1512 to 2488 code bytes
//      and gained 24 `.data` slots. `StdlibFacts.unreachable` is what closes it, and it is stated as
//      reachability rather than as membership precisely because the fix outlives this file.
//
// ⭐ THE COLLISION RULE FOR ADDING AN ENTRY. A listed module must declare no name a user program
// declares and no builtin type/name.
//   • FUNCTION-name collisions — a user `Clock.nowMs` against the stdlib one, or two stdlib modules —
//     are caught LOUDLY by the whole-program duplicate-function check (E3006, `commitFuncSignatures` in
//     ParseStaging), because a stdlib file merges through the identical path a user file does. Nothing
//     to add here; `stdlib-whitelist.md` pins it.
//   • TYPE-name-vs-builtin collisions are the maintainer's responsibility. shv2 has no
//     builtin-type-redeclaration diagnostic at all — a user `type String` compiles today as a distinct
//     nominal — so enforcing one is a general language matter, not a scaffolding one. Clock declares
//     `Clock`/`WallClock` and time typealiases, none of them builtin, so it cannot hit this. Do not list
//     a module that redeclares a builtin.
//   • ⚠ FUNCTION-name-vs-BARE-BUILTIN collisions are SILENT — and this is why `stdlib/Sleep.maxon` is
//     NOT listed, though the compiler can now compile it. `Parser.parseCallNamed` recognizes a handful
//     of BARE names (`print`, `sleep`, `trunc`, `runProcess`, the math intrinsics) BEFORE `emitCall`
//     consults any registry, so a CALL to such a name never reaches a declaration of it. Listing Sleep
//     would therefore load a module no ordinary call site can reach — `sleep(5)` still emits the
//     builtin's `__gt_sleep` — while charging the per-module load cost (MEASURED at +677 allocations
//     and ~30 KB on EVERY compile, flat across a 32x scale ladder) for zero delivered capability.
//     Worse, the name would have TWO routes: `let f = sleep` is not a call site, so it takes the
//     address of the stdlib `sleep`, whose body reaches the same entry the long way round.
//     ⚠ The shadowing is NOT this file's doing and predates it: a user file declaring its own
//     `function sleep` already compiles with the declaration silently unlinked, no diagnostic (measured
//     on plain user code). The repair is to delete the bare-name builtin and let the stdlib declaration
//     be authoritative — its own rung, because it moves every committed golden that calls `sleep`
//     (COUNTED: 15 goldens across 7 specs — async-sleep, async-promise-drop, async-stack-growth,
//     async-subprocess, builtins-clock, spawn-read-line, streaming-subprocess) and changes
//     `async-sleep.float-arg-rejected`'s pinned stderr.
//     ⇒ THE RULE: do not list a module whose function name a bare builtin already claims. Retire the
//     builtin first.

// The stdlib modules this compile will load, in the order it loads them. A DISTINCTLY-named alias
// rather than QueryDatabase's `FilePathArray`, whose name collides with a member typealias inside
// stdlib's `type Directory` and so does not resolve from every compiler file (`DiagnosticArray`, the
// same `Array with T` shape under a unique name, resolves cross-file fine — the name is the difference).
typealias StdlibModulePathArray = Array with FilePath

// A position in that array. The sort below shuffles these rather than the paths (see `sortedStdlibModules`).
typealias StdlibModuleIndex = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias StdlibModuleIndexArray = Array with StdlibModuleIndex

// ⭐ **THE LOADER.** Enumerate `stdlib/`, order the result, register each module. Called by `loadProject`
// AFTER the user's own sources are loaded (so a directory build's output name still derives from a user
// file, not from a stdlib module, and an empty user project still fails with `noSourceFiles` rather than
// silently compiling only stdlib).
//
// ⚠ LOUD, NEVER SILENT. An unreadable module propagates `readError` naming it; a PARSE failure surfaces
// through the ordinary parse-diagnostic path (`queryParseOps` → `reportParseError`), which already
// renders the file path. There is no arm that drops a stdlib module quietly.
export function loadStdlibSources(project Project) throws CompileError
	// ⬇ WHITELIST (1 of 3) — the list, and the check that it names files this checkout actually has.
	// Delete these two lines with the rest of the filter.
	let listed = try whitelistedStdlibModules(project.stdlibDir)

	var modules = StdlibModulePathArray.create()
	try collectStdlibModules(project.stdlibDir, listed: listed, into: modules)
	let ordered = sortedStdlibModules(modules)

	for path in ordered 'each'
		try loadOneSourceFile(project, path: path)
		// RECORDED, because it is the answer to "which of this compile's files are stdlib source?" and
		// this loop is the one place that knows it for free — every path here came out of an enumeration
		// of `stdlib/`. Re-deriving it later by testing each source path against that directory costs
		// four String allocations a question (see StdlibSource's header).
		project.stdlibSourcePaths.push(path)
	end 'each'
end 'loadStdlibSources'

// Every `.maxon` file under `dir`, descending subdirectories (`stdlib/helpers/**` is stdlib too).
function collectStdlibModules(dir FilePath, listed StdlibModulePathArray, into StdlibModulePathArray) throws CompileError
	let entries = try Directory.list(dir) otherwise throw CompileError.directoryNotFound(dir)
	for entry in entries 'each'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'isSubdir'
			try collectStdlibModules(entry, listed: listed, into: into)
		end 'isSubdir' else if entry.filename().endsWith(StdlibSourceExtension) 'isModule'
			// ⬇ WHITELIST (2 of 3) — the ONLY thing between this loader and all of `stdlib/`.
			// Delete this `if` and every stdlib module is loaded.
			if not isWhitelistedStdlibModule(entry, listed: listed) 'notListed'
				continue
			end 'notListed'
			into.push(entry)
		end 'isModule'
	end 'each'
end 'collectStdlibModules'

// The extension a stdlib module carries. Named here rather than shared with `Compiler.maxon`'s
// user-source constant because they answer to different owners: one is what a USER project's files are
// named, the other is what `stdlib/` contains, and a day that changes one need not change the other.
let StdlibSourceExtension = ".maxon"

// Order the modules byte-lexicographically by full path, so the load order — and therefore the emitted
// function order and every committed golden — is the same on every machine rather than whatever order
// the filesystem handed back. Insertion sort: it runs over the FILTERED list, so today (one module) it
// does no comparisons at all, and in the end state it is a few thousand byte-compares over a fixed-size
// directory, once per compile. Nothing depends on WHICH order this is, only on its being stable (see
// this file's header on why shv2, unlike the bootstrap, needs no semantic ordering).
//
// ⚠ It sorts an INDEX column and materializes the paths once at the end, rather than swapping the paths
// in place. Reading an element out of an array while writing that same array is a read-borrow held
// across a mutation, which the ownership checker refuses (E3070) — correctly, since the write may
// reallocate what the read borrowed. An index is a scalar copy, so the column can be shuffled freely.
function sortedStdlibModules(modules StdlibModulePathArray) returns StdlibModulePathArray
	var order = StdlibModuleIndexArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto modules.count() 'seed'
		order.push(i)
	end 'seed'

	for i in 1 upto order.count() 'place'
		let moved = try order.get(i) otherwise panic("sortedStdlibModules: order.get({i}) below its own count()")
		var j = i
		while j > 0 'shift'
			let prior = try order.get(j - 1) otherwise panic("sortedStdlibModules: order.get({j}-1) below its own count()")
			if not pathSortsBefore(moduleAt(modules, index: moved), other: moduleAt(modules, index: prior)) 'inPlace'
				break
			end 'inPlace'
			try order.set(j, value: prior) otherwise panic("sortedStdlibModules: order.set({j}) below its own count()")
			j = j - 1
		end 'shift'
		try order.set(j, value: moved) otherwise panic("sortedStdlibModules: order.set({j}) below its own count()")
	end 'place'

	var sorted = StdlibModulePathArray.create()
	for index in order 'emit'
		sorted.push(moduleAt(modules, index: index))
	end 'emit'
	return sorted
end 'sortedStdlibModules'

// One module path by index. The bounds failure is impossible — every index in `order` was seeded from
// `modules`' own count — so it panics rather than inventing a path.
function moduleAt(modules StdlibModulePathArray, index StdlibModuleIndex) returns FilePath
	return try modules.get(index) otherwise panic("moduleAt: index {index} is outside the {modules.count()} collected modules")
end 'moduleAt'

// Byte-lexicographic `a < b` over two paths: the first differing byte decides, and a proper prefix
// sorts first. Written here because `String` has no ordering comparison — only `equals` — and a total
// order is the whole requirement.
function pathSortsBefore(a FilePath, other FilePath) returns bool
	let left = a.toString().toByteArray()
	let right = other.toString().toByteArray()
	let shared = left.count() if left.count() < right.count() else right.count()

	for i in 0 upto shared 'compare'
		let lb = try left.get(i) otherwise panic("pathSortsBefore: left.get({i}) below the shared length")
		let rb = try right.get(i) otherwise panic("pathSortsBefore: right.get({i}) below the shared length")
		if lb != rb 'differs'
			return lb < rb
		end 'differs'
	end 'compare'

	return left.count() < right.count()
end 'pathSortsBefore'

// ============================================================================================
// ⬇ WHITELIST (3 of 3) — THE WHOLE OF THE SCAFFOLDING. Everything above this line is the real
// loader and stays. Delete from here down, plus the two marked call sites, and shv2 loads all of
// `stdlib/`.
// ============================================================================================

// The stdlib modules this rung loads, as paths RELATIVE to the checkout root (the parent of `stdlib/`).
// Resolved against the located directory so both sides of the membership test below are built the same
// way — `FilePath.join` normalizes separators, so a `/`-spelled entry matches an enumerated `\` path.
// A listed module the checkout does not have is a hard error naming it, never a silent skip: the whole
// point of the list is that what it names is present.
function whitelistedStdlibModules(stdlibDir FilePath) returns StdlibModulePathArray throws CompileError
	let root = try stdlibDir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound

	var listed = StdlibModulePathArray.create()
	listed.push(root.join("stdlib/Clock.maxon"))

	for path in listed 'exists'
		if not File.exists(path) 'missing'
			throw CompileError.stdlibModuleMissing(path)
		end 'missing'
	end 'exists'
	return listed
end 'whitelistedStdlibModules'

function isWhitelistedStdlibModule(path FilePath, listed StdlibModulePathArray) returns bool
	for entry in listed 'each'
		if entry.equals(path) 'match'
			return true
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'isWhitelistedStdlibModule'
